A||N||13||mope||~what should i know when buying a 85 gt?~||||5||
Z||000000||mope||09-20-2000||06:21 PM||||Im gunna get my first fiero in the next coming weeks and it is going to be a red 85 gt. Perfect inside and out. 53,000 miles. Garaged last 6 years. The air doesnt work (i dont know why yet) and it spun a rod bearing. Is there anything about the 85 that i should stay away from?<BR>What should i expect to pay for a car of this quality?<BR>Im probly just gunna get it nomatter what i end up paying for it but im just wondering if im getting ripped off. <BR>Im sure everybody will be seeing more of me on this forum once i buy my fiero.<P>thanks, see ya all later<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||DJRice||09-20-2000||08:12 PM||||There are lots of 85GTs around and I think that overall people like them.  I cant think of any specific problems with that model. <P>However, the spun bearing indicates that someone was kicking the RPMs up like they were going out of style.  <P>In fact, if you lived in North or Central Fla I would tell you to stay away from it and then ask for the address!!!<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000002||mope||09-20-2000||11:11 PM||||any idea what i should pay for it?||||reg||5
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||09-20-2000||11:59 PM||||  Spun rod?  figure on rebuilding or finding another 2.8L V6 soon.  likely you'll need to invest anywhere from $500 to $700 depending on the shop prices in your area just to get the engine in running condition.  I'm taking a REAL ball park, out in left field guess with no pictures, don't know the options on the car, and not knowing if it's a manual or auto.  <P>Just by going on what info you've given I'd expect a price somewhere between $1000 to $1500 with the motor in that condition.  A good running 85GT is priced around $2000 to $2500 around here.  Showroom condition $3000.<P>Please don't swear by my numbers, I'm going on limited info & history on the car.  Check auto swappers, newspapers, and used dealers to get an idea of the going prices in your area.  Hope this helps, happy Fiero hunting!<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">        <BR>        <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||09-21-2000||09:03 AM||||I would tend to agree with those numbers, and expect to throw $1K in the engine to get it back on the road.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||09-21-2000||09:52 AM||||I have an 85GT and an 85SE v-6.  I don't think there are any peculiarities with the 85 that you don't find in any other Fiero.<P>As with all Fieros check the following:<P>E-brake<BR>headlights<BR>A/C<BR>EGR tube<BR>Does everything electrical work?<BR>Does it pull when stopping?<BR>Any vibrations at speed?<BR>Does it feel squirly while driving (meaning does it feel like the back end is trying to pass the front end - if so needs 4 wheel alignment)<BR>Does it run hot in stop and go traffic?<BR>Does the radiator/trunk fan come on when the A/C switch is on?<BR>Headrest speakers work?<BR>If a 4 spd, does it have problems shifting between 3rd and 4th? (shift lever problems - not hard to fix)<BR>Clutch work properly?<BR>If auto does the TCC lock up properly?<P>Check out Oliver's page for a pre-flight check list at:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> <P>Good luck and welcome to the world of Fieros.<P>Carroll<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mope||09-25-2000||09:27 PM||||I was spost be goin to take a look at it last sunday (24th) but the guy i was getting it from wasnt gunna be there so i hafta make it for this following sunday. Anyone else have any good input about the 85 for me?<P>I was thinking about putting a fastback conversion on it (personaly think it much cooler...is it hard to do?....is it worth doing? Also, what is everyones standpoint on the 3 inch archie chop like on the purple pavement eater?? I think i want that done too.<P>See ya all round||||reg||2
Z||000007||Old Lar||09-25-2000||09:53 PM||||The 85 GT is the only notch back model GT so can be thought of as special.  It was reported to be the fastest GT model although all the equipment, chips were supposed to be the same.  The conversion to the fast back is relatively easy, I've been told.  I have two fast back GT s, but I don't know if I were you I'd change the style.  You'll be putting enough $ into engine repairs plus other stuff I'm sure you will find broken.<P>I got my 88 GT with 54K miles (in storage 4 years) on it and three years later, I've replaced suspension, steering rack, alternator, upper control arms, ball joints, e brake repair, a/c fixed then switched to 134a with a used compressor, exhaust manifold, exhaust system new paint, steering wheel, sail panels, head liner, radio, speakers, two fuel pumps, fuel rail, torque convertor selinoid, sensors up the yinyang, tires, gold lace wheels, distributor, two set of wires, a 3.1 engine, (now getting a 3.4) and new transmission.<P>So if the car has been "beat" in any way, you will have plenty of things to buy for your 85 GT with 53000 miles.||||reg||11
Z||000008||mope||09-29-2000||04:39 AM||||Wish me luck on a good buy.<P>Im goin to see it this sunday, im sure when i get back from seein it i will be on here to list everything about it so i can get some help  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>This forum is fun.<P>See you all late sunday or monday mornin.<P>Mope~||||reg||10
Z||000009||2xsess||09-29-2000||05:44 AM||||If it is a standard check the clutch pedal to see if it is bent, they had the imfamous aluminum pedals. But pretty much what everyone else said, if you need to, make a list of things you want to check. <P>GOOD LUCK<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||mope||10-01-2000||09:11 PM||||Well...where to start...<P>I tool my 3 hour drive to go see it and this is what happened.<P>Turns out its not a GT.<BR>its a 85 se coupe (2m6)(not what i wanted)<BR>interior is pretty good<BR>paint looked good(through the inch of dust anyway)<BR>was an automatic<BR>not original wheels <BR>nothing else really to say about it except that i am not getting it and am very sad about that...anyone with a 85-87 gt please let me know, i will be posting another topic asking for info on ones for sale in my area.<P>See ya all later.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000011||Heisenhund||10-01-2000||09:49 PM||||You mentioned maybe putting a fastback kit on an 85 GT. I have an 85 and an 86 GT and here's my .02<P><OL TYPE=A><BR><LI> Fastback looks more modern, notchback looks more Fiero.<P><LI> Engine is harder to work on from the top on the fastback (leaning over that clip is quite a pain).<BR></OL><P>But the main reason my 86 is my driver is that it has the 5 speed & gets better gas mileage than the automatic equipped 85.<P>Heis<P>If it was me, I might have gone with the SE. Wouldn't take much to make it look like a GT. Alas, I already have an 85...I need an 84, 87 and 88 still.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||10-02-2000||10:39 AM||||Mope,<P>I have a 80,000 mi 85 GT in S.W. Ohio that is for sale.  I and several other members have driven the car (Chadman, fiero56, fiero87ec05, Bengal4 and Boilermaker?).  It is absolutely the strongest running Fiero that I have ever driven.  The cosmetics are a slightly different story.  <P>Let me know if you want any more info/testimonials.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
